{
  "cards": [
    {
      "question": "When you shine dim light through two tiny slits onto a wall, the pattern looks like…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Two bright vertical lines",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A blurry central glow",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Alternating bright and dark bands",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Alternating bright and dark bands. Even individual photons interfere with themselves like water waves, creating stripes where crests meet and dark gaps where they cancel out."
    },
    {
      "question": "In the quantum world, an electron trapped inside an atom can orbit…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Only at specific fixed energies",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "At any smooth, continuous distance",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Wherever gravity pulls it",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Only at specific fixed energies. Atomic energy levels are quantized into distinct rungs like a ladder, meaning the electron can never hover between them."
    },
    {
      "question": "If you cool a box to absolute zero, the particles inside will…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Stop moving entirely",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Still jiggle with baseline energy",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Collapse into the center",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Still jiggle with baseline energy. Heisenberg's uncertainty principle forbids a particle from having zero momentum and a fixed position at the same time, leaving behind zero-point energy."
    },
    {
      "question": "When a particle meets an energy barrier it lacks the power to climb, it can…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Vanish permanently",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Pop out on the other side",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Split into two smaller pieces",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Pop out on the other side. Through quantum tunneling, a particle's wave function leaks through the barrier, giving it a real chance of appearing on the far side without climbing over."
    },
    {
      "question": "The laser scanner reading a barcode at the grocery store works by using photons that are…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Identical in wavelength and phase",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Heated to extreme temperatures",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Bound tightly like atomic nuclei",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Identical in wavelength and phase. Stimulated emission coaxes atoms to release clones of incoming light waves, producing a coherent, focused beam."
    },
    {
      "question": "According to the uncertainty principle, precisely pinning down an electron's position makes its…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Electrical charge disappear",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Mass multiply rapidly",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Speed and direction unpredictable",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
