{
  "cards": [
    {
      "question": "To speed up a grandfather clock that runs five minutes slow, you should…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Add weight to the pendulum",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Raise the pendulum bob",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Wind the mainspring tighter",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Raise the pendulum bob. A shorter swing distance takes less time, so screwing the rating nut upward speeds up the clock. Changing its weight does not alter the swing period."
    },
    {
      "question": "Before taking apart an old spring-driven clock movement, you must always…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Let down the mainsprings",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Soak the gears in kerosene",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Remove the dial hands",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Let down the mainsprings. A wound mainspring stores enormous mechanical tension. Loosening the plates while it holds power can shatter gear teeth or injure your fingers."
    },
    {
      "question": "When oiling a restored clock movement, you place the lubricant…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Along the teeth of all gears",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Only in the pivot sink cups",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Across the entire brass plate",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Only in the pivot sink cups. Putting oil on gear teeth attracts dust and grit, forming an abrasive paste that wears out the metal gears quickly."
    },
    {
      "question": "Restorers use pegwood, a small stick of soft dogwood, primarily to…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Scrape dried crud from pivot holes",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Measure gear tooth spacing",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Apply finish to wooden cabinets",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Scrape dried crud from pivot holes. Whittled pegwood absorbs old gummy oil and abrasive grit from tiny brass bearing holes without scratching the metal."
    },
    {
      "question": "Over decades, a steel gear axle spinning in a brass hole usually causes wear to…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "The harder steel axle",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "The softer brass pivot hole",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Both metals equally",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "The softer brass pivot hole. The constant sideways force widens the round hole into an egg shape, which restorers fix by pressing in a new brass bushing."
    },
    {
      "question": "The rhythmic tick-tock of a mechanical pendulum clock is produced by…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "The escape wheel hitting the pallets",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "The pendulum bob cutting the air",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Gears grinding against each other",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "The escape wheel hitting the pallets. Each tick is an escape tooth arresting against a pallet arm, which also gives the pendulum a tiny push to keep swinging."
    },
