      "question": "When a pendulum clock is slightly out of beat, it…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Runs twice as fast",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Has an uneven rhythm and stalls",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Strikes the chime constantly",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Has an uneven rhythm and stalls. An uneven tick-tock means the impulse reaches the pendulum late on one side, which soon robs it of momentum until it halts."
    },
    {
      "question": "A clock that counts the hours with a count wheel differs from a rack-and-pinion chime because it…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Uses electrical contacts",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Never needs winding",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Strikes out of order if nudged",
          "correct": true
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Strikes out of order if nudged. Count wheels step rigidly through a fixed sequence, so moving the hands backward makes the chime count disagree with the face."
    },
    {
      "question": "Old brass clock plates often develop dark tarnish because the copper in them reacts with…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "Airborne sulfur and moisture",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Pure nitrogen in the case",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Sunlight through the glass",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "Airborne sulfur and moisture. Sulfur gases in ambient air bind with copper atoms in brass, creating a dark patina that conservators clean with gentle solvent baths."
    },
    {
      "question": "A deadbeat escapement was considered a major breakthrough over the older recoil type because…",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "It eliminated clock oil entirely",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "The gear train never jumps backward",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "It worked without a swinging pendulum",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "reveal": "The gear train never jumps backward. Unlike recoil escapements that push the escape wheel in reverse on each swing, deadbeat pallets let it rest motionless."
    }
  ]
}
